    <Section title="Filters"/>
    <SubSection title="Why Is Filtering Important?" id="Applications">
        <p>Filters can be found in almost any electronic device, and especially so in medical devices which handle lots of data and measurements. The primary goal of filtering in any system is to get rid of noise while preserving important data for use by physicians. This improves the <a href="/signals/signal_to_noise_ratio?origin=coursemenu">signal to noise ratio (SNR)</a> and makes signals more useful and easier to interpret for both people and other machines.</p>
        <p>For example, filters are very useful in ECG readings. While the goal of an ECG is to detect electrical impulses from the heart rhythm, many other electrical signals can interfere such as muscle movements, including breathing, and even 60Hz AC currents nearby. Noisy signals are more difficult to understand and may even prove fatal if a physician cannot quickly and accurately diagnose a patient in events such as a heart attack.</p>
    </SubSection>
    
    <SubSection title="What Are Filters And How Are They Applied?" id="How">
        <p>Filters are applied to frequency domain signals (studied in the <a href="/signals/fourier_transform?origin=coursemenu">Fourier Transform</a>) by attenuating (reducing the amplitude of) sine waves within that range of frequencies. Different types of filters such as low pass, high pass, band pass, and band stop filters determine the range of frequencies which are attenuated. In linear system analysis, filters are also commonly designed and analyzed using their s-domain <a href="/signals/transfer_functions?origin=coursemenu">Transfer Functions</a>.</p>
        <Image src="/signals/filtertypes.png"><span class="fw-bold">Figure 1.</span> Examples of the most common signal filter types. Depicted are ideal filters with an x-axis of frequency, usually in Hertz (Hz) and y-axis of gain, usually in volts (V) or decibels (dB). (<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/monograph/abs/pii/B9780750650489500064">image source</a>)</Image>
        <br>
        <p>The example filters above are ideal filters, but because filters are made from combinations of physical electrical components, in reality, they do not have perfect attenuation at the cutoff frequency <InlineEquation equation="f_c"/>.</p>
        <p>The ability for a filter to begin acting within its cutoff frequency, or its quality, is measured by its Q factor. An example of a more realistic, imperfect filter is shown below.</p>
        <Image src="/signals/realisticfilter.png"><span class="fw-bold">Figure 2.</span> A realistic filter cannot completely attenuate unwanted frequencies and cannot transition from unaffected frequencies to attenuated frequencies instantaneously, losing some desired signals and retaining some unwanted signals.</Image>
        <p>The cutoff frequency of a filter is defined as the frequency at which the filter begins attenuating a signal at -3dB. Bandpass and bandstop filters have two cutoff frequencies, one high, and one low, which define the "band" within which the filter acts. The cutoff frequency for each filter can also be calculated using the values of the electrical components that make it up, the exact formulas are described <a href="#Types">below</a>.</p>
        <Image src="/signals/cutoff.jpg"><span class="fw-bold">Figure 3.</span> The frequency at which the filter intersects with the -3dB line on the y-axis is the cutoff frequency (<InlineEquation equation="f_c"/>) of this low-pass filter. (<a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/monograph/pii/B9780750672337500065">image source</a>)</Image>
    </SubSection>
    
    <SubSection title="Gain and Decibels" id="Gain">
        <p>How much the signal is attenuated is measured in voltage gain or decibel gain where voltage gain is a linear ratio of the amplitude of a signal before and after attenuation/amplification: <InlineEquation equation="\\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}"/> with a unit of V/V and decibels are: <InlineEquation equation="dB = 20\\log_{10}\\left({\\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}}\\right)"/> in relation to the gain with a unit of dB.</p>
        <p>When a signal is amplified it has a voltage gain of greater than 1 and a positive decibel gain, and when it is attenuated it has a voltage gain between 0 and 1 and a negative decibel gain.</p>
        <Example id="gain_ex" title="Find the signal voltage after a decibel gain is applied to it." solution="True">
            <div class="d-flex flex-row">
                <p>A 16V signal is attenuated by a filter of -40 dB. What is the voltage/amplitude of the signal afterward?</p>
            </div>
            <div slot="solution">
                <p>First convert the decibel gain into a voltage gain, this way it can be multiplied with the signal voltage directly. Using the equation:</p>
                <DisplayEquation title="" equation="dB = 20\\log_{10}\\left({\\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}}\\right)"/>
                <p>Plug in the values and solve:</p>
                <DisplayEquation title="" equation="-40 = 20\\log_{10}\\left({\\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}}\\right)"/>
                <DisplayEquation title="" equation="-2 = \\log_{10}\\left({\\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}}\\right)"/>
                <DisplayEquation title="" equation="10^{-2} = \\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}"/>
                <DisplayEquation title="" equation="0.01 = \\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}"/>
                <p>The voltage gain is 0.01V/V. Multiply the initial signal amplitude of 16V by 0.01V/V for a final amplitude of 0.16V.</p>
            </div>
        </Example>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection title="Bode Plots: Characterizing Filter Frequency Response" id="BodePlots">
        <p>In physical circuits and biomedical instruments, ideal "brick-wall" filters cannot be built because causal physical systems cannot instantaneously drop gain to zero without infinite delay. As emphasized in biomedical instrumentation design:</p>
        
        <div class="alert alert-info border-start border-4 border-info d-flex align-items-center gap-3 my-3 p-3 rounded" role="alert">
            <i class="bi bi-graph-up fs-2 text-info"></i>
            <div>
                <strong class="fs-5">Whiteboard Principle:</strong><br>
                <em>"Bode Plot: Gives us a representation of the actual filter."</em>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p>A <strong>Bode plot</strong> is a graphical representation of the transfer function <InlineEquation equation="H(j\omega)"/> of a linear time-invariant (LTI) system as a function of frequency. It consists of two complementary plots sharing a common logarithmic frequency horizontal axis (<InlineEquation equation="\omega"/> in rad/s or <InlineEquation equation="f"/> in Hz):</p>

        <Enumerate>
            <Item>
                <strong>Bode Magnitude Plot:</strong> Plots filter gain in decibels (<InlineEquation equation="|H(j\omega)|_{\text{dB}} = 20\log_{10}|H(j\omega)| = 20\log_{10}(V_{\text{out}}/V_{\text{in}})"/>) versus frequency on a <InlineEquation equation="\log_{10}"/> scale.
            </Item>
            <Item>
                <strong>Bode Phase Plot:</strong> Plots the output-to-input phase angle / phase shift (<InlineEquation equation="\angle H(j\omega)"/> in degrees or radians) versus frequency on a <InlineEquation equation="\log_{10}"/> scale.
            </Item>
        </Enumerate>

        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">Why Logarithmic Frequency and Decibel Scales?</h4>
        <p>The logarithmic axes offer two decisive advantages in biomedical signal analysis:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Wide Frequency Span:</strong> Biomedical signals span several decades of frequency&mdash;from quasi-DC baseline wander (0.05 Hz) and ECG complexes (0.05&ndash;100 Hz), to EMG action potentials (10&ndash;500 Hz) and ultrasound (1&ndash;15 MHz). A logarithmic scale allows all of these physiological phenomena to be displayed and analyzed on a single chart without crowding low frequencies into the origin.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Linear Asymptotes and Cascade Additivity:</strong> On logarithmic frequency axes, the asymptotic roll-off of physical poles and zeros becomes simple straight lines. Furthermore, because <InlineEquation equation="\log(A \cdot B) = \log(A) + \log(B)"/>, cascading multi-stage filter blocks allows their decibel gains and phase shifts to simply be <em>added</em> together graphically.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">Filter Roll-Off and Filter Order</h4>
        <p>The steepness with which a filter attenuates unwanted frequencies beyond the cutoff frequency <InlineEquation equation="f_c"/> is called the <strong>roll-off rate</strong> (or attenuation slope). In a Bode magnitude plot, roll-off is expressed in <strong>decibels per decade (dB/decade)</strong> or <strong>decibels per octave (dB/octave)</strong>:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                A <strong>decade</strong> is a tenfold increase in frequency (<InlineEquation equation="f_2 / f_1 = 10"/>).
            </li>
            <li>
                An <strong>octave</strong> is a doubling of frequency (<InlineEquation equation="f_2 / f_1 = 2"/>).
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p>Because <InlineEquation equation="20\log_{10}(10) = 20\text{ dB}"/> and <InlineEquation equation="20\log_{10}(2) \approx 6.02\text{ dB}"/>, each <InlineEquation equation="-20\text{ dB/decade}"/> corresponds approximately to <InlineEquation equation="-6\text{ dB/octave}"/>. Each reactive energy-storage element (capacitor or inductor)&mdash;and hence each pole in the transfer function&mdash;contributes an additional <InlineEquation equation="-20\text{ dB/decade}"/> to the high-frequency asymptotic slope:</p>

        <div class="row g-3 my-2">
            <div class="col-md-4">
                <div class="card h-100 p-3 border shadow-sm bg-light-subtle">
                    <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary mb-1">1st-Order Filter (<InlineEquation equation="n = 1"/>)</h6>
                    <ul class="small mb-0 ps-3">
                        <li>Roll-off: <strong class="text-danger">-20 dB/decade</strong> (-6 dB/octave)</li>
                        <li>Phase at <InlineEquation equation="f_c"/>: <InlineEquation equation="-45^\circ"/></li>
                        <li>High-frequency phase: <InlineEquation equation="-90^\circ"/></li>
                        <li>Example: Single passive RC filter</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
                <div class="card h-100 p-3 border shadow-sm bg-light-subtle">
                    <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary mb-1">2nd-Order Filter (<InlineEquation equation="n = 2"/>)</h6>
                    <ul class="small mb-0 ps-3">
                        <li>Roll-off: <strong class="text-danger">-40 dB/decade</strong> (-12 dB/octave)</li>
                        <li>Phase at <InlineEquation equation="f_c"/>: <InlineEquation equation="-90^\circ"/></li>
                        <li>High-frequency phase: <InlineEquation equation="-180^\circ"/></li>
                        <li>Example: Sallen-Key biquad, RLC filter</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
                <div class="card h-100 p-3 border shadow-sm bg-light-subtle">
                    <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary mb-1"><InlineEquation equation="n"/>th-Order Filter</h6>
                    <ul class="small mb-0 ps-3">
                        <li>Roll-off: <strong class="text-danger">-20n dB/decade</strong> (-6n dB/octave)</li>
                        <li>Phase at <InlineEquation equation="f_c"/>: <InlineEquation equation="-n \times 45^\circ"/> (Butterworth)</li>
                        <li>High-frequency phase: <InlineEquation equation="-n \times 90^\circ"/></li>
                        <li>Formed by cascading <InlineEquation equation="n/2"/> biquad stages</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

