        <Example id="bode_rolloff_ex" title="Calculating Noise Attenuation Using Filter Roll-Off" solution="True">
            <div class="d-flex flex-row">
                <p>An ECG amplifier incorporates a <strong>2nd-order low-pass Butterworth filter</strong> with a cutoff frequency of <InlineEquation equation="f_c = 6\text{ Hz}"/> to remove unwanted 60 Hz AC powerline hum while preserving low-frequency cardiovascular rhythms. Calculate how many decibels of attenuation the filter provides at 60 Hz, and find the percentage of power remaining in the 60 Hz interference.</p>
            </div>
            <div slot="solution">
                <p><strong>Step 1: Calculate the frequency ratio in decades.</strong></p>
                <p>The interference frequency <InlineEquation equation="f = 60\text{ Hz}"/> is compared to the cutoff frequency <InlineEquation equation="f_c = 6\text{ Hz}"/>:</p>
                <DisplayEquation equation="\frac{f}{f_c} = \frac{60\text{ Hz}}{6\text{ Hz}} = 10 \implies 1\text{ decade above cutoff}"/>
                
                <p class="mt-2"><strong>Step 2: Apply the asymptotic roll-off formula for a 2nd-order filter.</strong></p>
                <p>For an <InlineEquation equation="n"/>th-order filter, the roll-off slope is <InlineEquation equation="-20n\text{ dB/decade}"/>. For <InlineEquation equation="n = 2"/>:</p>
                <DisplayEquation equation="\text{Attenuation Rate} = -20(2) = -40\text{ dB/decade}"/>
                <p>Since 60 Hz is exactly 1 decade above 6 Hz, the attenuation is approximately:</p>
                <DisplayEquation equation="\text{Gain}(60\text{ Hz}) \approx -40\text{ dB}"/>

                <p class="mt-2"><strong>Step 3: Convert decibels to voltage ratio and power ratio.</strong></p>
                <DisplayEquation equation="-40\text{ dB} = 20\log_{10}\left(\frac{V_{\text{out}}}{V_{\text{in}}}\right) \implies \frac{V_{\text{out}}}{V_{\text{in}}} = 10^{-40/20} = 10^{-2} = 0.01\text{ V/V}"/>
                <p>The voltage amplitude is reduced to 1% of its original value. Since power is proportional to voltage squared:</p>
                <DisplayEquation equation="\frac{P_{\text{out}}}{P_{\text{in}}} = \left(\frac{V_{\text{out}}}{V_{\text{in}}}\right)^2 = (0.01)^2 = 0.0001 = 0.01\%"/>
                <p>The 2nd-order filter eliminates 99.99% of the 60 Hz powerline noise power while leaving frequencies below 6 Hz largely unaffected.</p>
            </div>
        </Example>
    </SubSection>

    <SubSection title="Classical Filter Approximations" id="Approximations">
        <p>In analog circuit synthesis and digital IIR filter design, an ideal "brick-wall" filter is physically impossible because its rectangular frequency response has an infinite, non-causal impulse response (the sinc function, extending from <InlineEquation equation="t = -\infty"/> to <InlineEquation equation="+\infty"/>). To achieve a causal, realizable filter, engineers use mathematical polynomial approximations that trade off four competing design objectives:</p>

        <div class="row g-2 my-2 text-center">
            <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
                <div class="p-2 border rounded bg-light-subtle h-100">
                    <strong class="text-primary d-block">1. Passband Flatness</strong>
                    <small class="text-muted">No ripple or distortion in desired signals</small>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
                <div class="p-2 border rounded bg-light-subtle h-100">
                    <strong class="text-primary d-block">2. Transition Steepness</strong>
                    <small class="text-muted">Sharp roll-off between passband &amp; stopband</small>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
                <div class="p-2 border rounded bg-light-subtle h-100">
                    <strong class="text-primary d-block">3. Stopband Attenuation</strong>
                    <small class="text-muted">Deep suppression of out-of-band interference</small>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
                <div class="p-2 border rounded bg-light-subtle h-100">
                    <strong class="text-primary d-block">4. Phase Linearity</strong>
                    <small class="text-muted">Constant group delay, zero pulse dispersion</small>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">The four classical approximations each optimize one or more of these tradeoffs for specific biomedical instruments:</p>

        <SubSubSection title="1. Bessel (Thomson) Filter: Maximally Flat Group Delay" id="BesselFilter">
            <p>The <strong>Bessel filter</strong> (developed by W. E. Thomson using Bessel polynomials) is optimized for <strong>linear phase response</strong> across its passband, which corresponds mathematically to a <strong>maximally flat group delay</strong>:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="\tau_g(\omega) = -\frac{d\phi(\omega)}{d\omega} \approx \text{constant}"/>
            <p><strong>Time-Domain Advantage:</strong> When all sinusoidal frequency components in a complex biological signal experience the exact same time delay through the filter, the signal preserves its shape without <em>phase dispersion</em>, overshoot, or ringing. Transient pulses, steep step transitions, and physiological wave contours pass through unaltered.</p>
            <p><strong>Frequency-Domain Trade-off:</strong> The Bessel filter has the gentlest (slowest) transition steepness among all classical filters. It rolls off far more slowly than a Butterworth or Chebyshev filter of the same order.</p>
            <CalloutCard title="Biomedical Application: Morphological Waveform Preservation">
                <p class="mb-1">Bessel filters are the gold standard for clinical signals where the <strong>exact time-domain shape and amplitude</strong> of a transient pulse carry life-or-death diagnostic information:</p>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Electrocardiography (ECG):</strong> Preserves subtle ST-segment elevation/depression (indicative of myocardial infarction) and sharp R-wave peaks without introducing artificial ringing that could mimic biphasic arrhythmias.</li>
                    <li><strong>Arterial Blood Pressure (ABP) &amp; Pulse Wave Velocity (PWV):</strong> Faithfully reproduces the systolic upstroke slope (<InlineEquation equation="dP/dt"/>) and the delicate dicrotic notch reflecting aortic valve closure.</li>
                    <li><strong>Electrophysiology Patch-Clamp &amp; Action Potentials:</strong> Preserves single-neuron action potential peak height and spike half-width during rapid depolarization.</li>
                </ul>
            </CalloutCard>
        </SubSubSection>

        <SubSubSection title="2. Butterworth Filter: Maximally Flat Passband Magnitude" id="ButterworthFilter">
            <p>The <strong>Butterworth filter</strong> is mathematically designed so that the first <InlineEquation equation="2n - 1"/> derivatives of its squared magnitude response are zero at <InlineEquation equation="\omega = 0"/>. Its magnitude function is expressed as:</p>
            <DisplayEquation equation="|H(j\omega)|^2 = \frac{1}{1 + \left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_c}\right)^{2n}}"/>
            <p><strong>Frequency-Domain Characteristics:</strong> Perfectly flat, monotonic magnitude response in the passband with <strong>zero ripple</strong> anywhere in the passband or stopband. The magnitude drops smoothly through exactly <InlineEquation equation="-3\text{ dB}"/> at the cutoff frequency <InlineEquation equation="\omega = \omega_c"/>, asymptotically rolling off at <InlineEquation equation="-20n\text{ dB/decade}"/>.</p>
            <p><strong>Time-Domain Trade-off:</strong> The phase response is moderately non-linear near the cutoff frequency, resulting in moderate group delay distortion and slight ringing on abrupt step transients.</p>
            <CalloutCard title="Biomedical Application: General-Purpose Bioinstrumentation Workhorse">
                <p class="mb-1">Because of its smooth response and complete absence of amplitude ripple, the Butterworth filter is the standard all-around filter across biomedical engineering:</p>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Electromyography (EMG):</strong> Filtering broadband surface muscle signals (10&ndash;500 Hz) where all frequencies in the passband must be weighted with uniform unity gain.</li>
                    <li><strong>General Bioelectric Signal Conditioning:</strong> Pre-filtering and band-limiting before analog-to-digital conversion (ADC) to prevent aliasing without corrupting signal amplitudes.</li>
                </ul>
            </CalloutCard>
        </SubSubSection>

        <SubSubSection title="3. Chebyshev Filter: Steeper Roll-Off via Permitted Ripple" id="ChebyshevFilter">
            <p>The <strong>Chebyshev filter</strong> (derived using Chebyshev polynomials <InlineEquation equation="T_n(\omega)"/>) achieves a significantly sharper roll-off between passband and stopband than a Butterworth filter of the same order. It accomplishes this steeper transition by permitting controlled ripple (equiripple oscillation) in one of its bands:</p>

            <div class="row g-3 my-2">
                <div class="col-md-6">
                    <div class="card h-100 p-3 border shadow-sm bg-light-subtle">
                        <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary mb-1">Chebyshev Type I (Standard Chebyshev)</h6>
                        <ul class="small mb-0 ps-3">
                            <li><strong>Passband:</strong> Equiripple oscillation (designer selects ripple level, e.g., 0.5 dB, 1 dB, or 3 dB).</li>
                            <li><strong>Stopband:</strong> Monotonic smooth decay (no stopband ripple).</li>
                            <li><strong>Advantage:</strong> Much steeper cutoff slope than Butterworth for the same order <InlineEquation equation="n"/>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Cost:</strong> Passband amplitude distortion and non-linear phase distortion near cutoff.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="col-md-6">
                    <div class="card h-100 p-3 border shadow-sm bg-light-subtle">
                        <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary mb-1">Chebyshev Type II (Inverse Chebyshev)</h6>
                        <ul class="small mb-0 ps-3">
                            <li><strong>Passband:</strong> Monotonically maximally flat (zero passband ripple, identical to Butterworth at low frequencies).</li>
                            <li><strong>Stopband:</strong> Equiripple lobes with transmission zeros.</li>
                            <li><strong>Advantage:</strong> Eliminates passband distortion while maintaining sharp roll-off.</li>
                            <li><strong>Cost:</strong> Finite stopband attenuation floor (stopband lobes).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <CalloutCard title="Biomedical Application: EEG Sub-Band Separation & Frequency Channelizers">
                <p class="mb-1">Chebyshev filters are prized when adjacent biomedical frequency channels sit very close together and must be sharply separated:</p>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Electroencephalography (EEG) Rhythms:</strong> Isolating tightly adjacent neurological brainwave bands: Delta (0.5&ndash;4 Hz), Theta (4&ndash;8 Hz), Alpha (8&ndash;13 Hz), Beta (13&ndash;30 Hz), and Gamma (&gt;30 Hz).</li>
                    <li><strong>Cochlear Implants &amp; Audiometry:</strong> Multi-channel filter banks decomposing acoustic speech into narrow frequency bins.</li>
                </ul>
            </CalloutCard>
        </SubSubSection>

        <SubSubSection title="4. Elliptic (Cauer) Filter: Steepest Transition Bandwidth" id="EllipticFilter">
            <p>The <strong>Elliptic filter</strong> (also known as the Cauer filter) uses Jacobian elliptic functions to distribute equiripple oscillations across <strong>both the passband and the stopband</strong> simultaneously. It incorporates finite transmission zeros (<InlineEquation equation="H(j\omega) = 0"/>) along the imaginary axis in the stopband.</p>
            <p><strong>Defining Advantage:</strong> The Elliptic filter provides the <strong>steepest possible transition</strong> from passband to stopband for any given filter order <InlineEquation equation="n"/>. It approaches the ideal brick-wall transition faster than any other classical polynomial approximation.</p>
            <p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> The phase response is extremely non-linear, group delay fluctuates wildly near cutoff, and step responses exhibit prolonged ringing and transient distortion.</p>
            <CalloutCard title="Biomedical Application: Ultra-Sharp Brick-Wall Notches & Narrow-Guard Anti-Aliasing">
                <p class="mb-1">Elliptic filters are deployed when transition space is at an absolute premium:</p>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Brick-Wall Powerline Notch Filters:</strong> Surgically removing 60 Hz hum with extremely narrow transition bands so that 58 Hz and 62 Hz biomedical rhythms are completely preserved.</li>
                    <li><strong>Anti-Aliasing with Tight Nyquist Guard Bands:</strong> In portable telemetry where sampling rates must be kept low to conserve battery power, an elliptic pre-filter cuts off frequencies right before the Nyquist limit <InlineEquation equation="f_s/2"/>.</li>
                </ul>
            </CalloutCard>
        </SubSubSection>

