    <SubSection title="Cascaded Second-Order Sections (Biquads)" id="Biquads">
        <p>When designing high-order active filters (<InlineEquation equation="n \ge 3"/>, such as a 4th-order or 6th-order Butterworth, Chebyshev, or Bessel filter), one could theoretically try to build a single op-amp circuit containing three or more capacitors in a complex feedback loop. However, in physical electronic design, this approach is avoided due to severe component sensitivity:</p>

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-start border-4 border-warning d-flex align-items-center gap-3 my-3 p-3 rounded" role="alert">
            <i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill fs-2 text-warning"></i>
            <div>
                <strong>Why Not Build a Single High-Order Op-Amp Circuit?</strong><br>
                In an <InlineEquation equation="n"/>th-order polynomial, the roots (poles) are extremely sensitive to component tolerance variations. A 1% drift in a single resistor or capacitor in a 6th-order single-stage circuit can shift pole locations drastically, causing peak resonance, distortion, or even pushing poles into the right-half plane (<InlineEquation equation="\sigma > 0"/>), causing spontaneous self-oscillation and circuit instability!
            </div>
        </div>

        <p>The universal, robust solution in both analog hardware and digital IIR filter design is to factor the high-order transfer function into a series cascade of <strong>Second-Order Sections (SOS)</strong>, commonly known as <strong>Biquads</strong> (biquadratic transfer functions):</p>

        <DisplayEquation equation="H(s) = H_1(s) \cdot H_2(s) \cdot H_3(s) \cdots H_k(s) = \prod_{i=1}^k \frac{b_{2i}s^2 + b_{1i}s + b_{0i}}{s^2 + \frac{\omega_{0i}}{Q_i}s + \omega_{0i}^2}"/>

        <p>For an even-order filter (e.g. <InlineEquation equation="n = 4"/> or <InlineEquation equation="n = 6"/>), the circuit consists of <InlineEquation equation="k = n / 2"/> biquad stages in series. For an odd-order filter (e.g. <InlineEquation equation="n = 3"/> or <InlineEquation equation="n = 5"/>), one simple 1st-order <InlineEquation equation="RC"/> stage is combined with <InlineEquation equation="(n-1)/2"/> biquad stages.</p>

        <div class="card p-3 my-3 border bg-light-subtle shadow-sm">
            <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary mb-2 text-center">Cascaded Second-Order Biquad Block Diagram</h6>
            <p class="text-muted small text-center mb-2">As highlighted on the lecture whiteboard: <InlineEquation equation="[\cdot] \to [\cdot] \to [\cdot]"/>. Factoring higher-order transfer functions into cascaded second-order sections isolates each conjugate pole pair, preventing instability and simplifying active op-amp implementation.</p>
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-center my-2">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 840 220" class="img-fluid rounded border bg-white p-2" style="max-width: 840px; width: 100%; height: auto;">
                    <defs>
