        <h4 class="fw-bold mt-4 mb-2">Key Engineering Advantages of Cascaded Biquad Sections:</h4>
        <Enumerate>
            <Item>
                <strong>Decoupled Pole Tuning:</strong> Each biquad section implements exactly one complex conjugate pole pair (<InlineEquation equation="s_{1,2} = -\frac{\omega_0}{2Q} \pm j\omega_0\sqrt{1 - \frac{1}{4Q^2}}"/>). Adjusting the resistor or capacitor values in stage 1 modifies only stage 1's pole pair, leaving the other poles completely unaffected.
            </Item>
            <Item>
                <strong>Proven Active Topologies:</strong> Each biquadratic section can be directly constructed using standard, highly reliable active op-amp topologies, most notably the <strong>Sallen-Key topology</strong> (non-inverting, low component count) or the <strong>Multiple-Feedback (MFB) topology</strong> (inverting, high Q stability).
            </Item>
            <Item>
                <strong>Optimal Dynamic Range &amp; Noise Sequencing:</strong> In physical circuits, cascading stages in order from <em>lowest Q to highest Q</em> prevents signal peaks from driving op-amps into saturation/clipping early in the signal chain, optimizing the signal-to-noise ratio across the entire instrument.
            </Item>
        </Enumerate>
    </SubSection>

